7 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527857 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527857 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186527530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527857 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7bb29f16-8d05-55d0-ab1d-19d482085ecc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527866 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527866 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527866 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527866 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186527430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527866 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8a43a5f5-8ea0-5a0e-95e4-95914ae5bdd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527924 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527924 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183347" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527924 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527924 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ If $x\\in (0,2),$ then $|x|<2.$\n\nWhich of the following is logically equivalent to the conditional predicate above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527924 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e5452f1-e9dc-544d-b364-2b179ccf8284" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527924 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote by $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ the following predicates:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x\\in (0,2).$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $|x|<2.$\n\nThen, our initial sentence can be written as $P(x)⇒Q(x),$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬P(x)∨Q(x).\n$$\n\nThe negation of $P(x)$ can be written as\n\n- $¬P(x):$ $x\\notin (0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the given sentence is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x\\notin (0,2),$ or $|x|<2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527961 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527961 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75975" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527961 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527961 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527961 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c3f2557-93da-5d6b-97fe-29c0805f0db0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527961 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,6\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,-3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=6\\cdot {b}_{1}+(-3)\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{2}=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527975 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527975 #edb/ref 1069 "q-274929" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527975 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527975 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $P$ is a $2\\times 5$ matrix, $Q$ is a $5\\times 7$ matrix, and $R$ is a $7\\times 2$ matrix, in which of the following expressions are the Hadamard products well-defined?\n\nI. $PQ⊙R$\n\nII. $PQ⊙{R}^{T}$\n\nIII. $(PQ)^{T}⊙R$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527975 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9c36f01c-b274-57ce-a233-5c5a47d4ef94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527975 #edb/ref 1074 "The Hadamard product of two matrices (or vectors) is only defined if they have the same dimensions.\n\nLet's consider each expression in turn:\n\n- The dimensions of $PQ$ are $2\\times 7,$ and the dimensions of $R$ are $7\\times 2.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression I is *not* well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $PQ$ are $2\\times 7,$ and the dimensions of ${R}^{T}$ are $2\\times 7.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression II is well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $(PQ)^{T}$ are $7\\times 2,$ and the dimensions of $R$ are $7\\times 2.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression III is well-defined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527991 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143431" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527991 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527991 #edb/ref 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2}$ of two pineapples is drawn from a population. The population has pineapples of two different weights, $1\\,\\text{kg}$ and $1.5\\,\\text{kg},$ distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527991 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c60b1a3b-1eee-5254-929b-6a532b619fd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527991 #edb/ref 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the pineapples in the population have weights $1\\,\\text{kg}$ and $\\text{1.5}\\,\\text{kg},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+4=5$ pineapples only with the following weights:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\text{kg},\\,1.5\\,\\text{kg},\\,1.5\\,\\text{kg},\\,1.5\\,\\text{kg},\\,1.5\\,\\text{kg}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+4\\cdot 1.5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5} \\\\  & =1.4\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({1}^{2}+4\\cdot {1.5}^{2})-{1.4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(10)-1.96 \\\\  & =2-1.96 \\\\  & =0.04\\,{\\text{kg}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{0.04}{2}=0.02\\,{\\text{kg}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.02}≈0.14\\,\\text{kg}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
